<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Phoenicia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Phoenicia was the ancient coastal region of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/levant">Levant</a>, roughly modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lebanon">Lebanon</a> with parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/syria">Syria</a> and northern Israel, home of city-states such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tyre">Tyre</a>, Sidon, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byblos">Byblos</a> and Arwad. From about 1200 BCE its merchants and sailors traded across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, founded colonies such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carthage">Carthage</a> and spread their alphabet. The region later fell under Assyrian, Persian, Hellenistic and Roman rule.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/phoenicia.jpg" alt="Phoenicia" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Kordas, based on Alvaro's work · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Phoenicia_map-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
